<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duke's Laws</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1665</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Duke's Laws were the legal code issued in 1665 for the territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england">James, Duke of York</a>, after the English capture of New Netherland. Drawn up under Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nicolls">Richard Nicolls</a> and first applied on Long Island, they allowed Protestant congregations to choose their own churches but provided for no elected assembly. They were gradually extended over the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and remained the basis of its law until the 1680s.</p></div>
